On iOS the backbuffer is not framebuffer zero.

    // Backbuffer for GL is a special case with a zero framebuffer.

says CFramebuffer::CreateBackbuffer, and everywhere else that is true. On iOS it
is not: a CAEAGLLayer is fed by a renderbuffer attached to a framebuffer object,
SDL creates that object together with the context, and its name is never zero.
Binding zero is legal and draws nowhere -- silently, at full speed.

That is what the engine did. It started, loaded the whole game, ran its frame
loop for minutes at a few frames a second, and every frame went into nothing;
the screen kept showing the simulator's placeholder. A bare clear-and-swap
against framebuffer zero came back black with GL_INVALID_ENUM, which is what
finally said it out loud -- while the GLES probe in this repository, which never
rebinds anything and leaves SDL's framebuffer in place, had been showing its
triangle all along.

So the device remembers what is bound right after the context is created, and
the backbuffer uses that instead of zero.

--- source/renderer/backend/gl/Device.h
+++ source/renderer/backend/gl/Device.h
@@ -137,11 +137,27 @@
 
 	const Capabilities& GetCapabilities() const override { return m_Capabilities; }
 
+public:
+#if OS_IOS
+	/**
+	 * The framebuffer the window draws through.
+	 *
+	 * Zero everywhere else, and on iOS anything but: a CAEAGLLayer is fed by a
+	 * renderbuffer attached to a framebuffer object, and SDL creates that
+	 * object when the context is made. Binding zero there draws nowhere at all
+	 * -- silently, at full speed, frame after frame.
+	 */
+	GLuint GetDefaultFramebuffer() const { return m_DefaultFramebuffer; }
+#endif
+
 private:
 	CDevice();
 
 	SDL_Window* m_Window = nullptr;
 	SDL_GLContext m_Context = nullptr;
+#if OS_IOS
+	GLuint m_DefaultFramebuffer = 0;
+#endif
 	int m_SurfaceDrawableWidth = 0, m_SurfaceDrawableHeight = 0;
 
 	bool m_ARB = false;
--- source/renderer/backend/gl/Device.cpp
+++ source/renderer/backend/gl/Device.cpp
@@ -243,6 +243,19 @@
 		}
 		SDL_GL_GetDrawableSize(window, &device->m_SurfaceDrawableWidth, &device->m_SurfaceDrawableHeight);
 
+#if OS_IOS
+		// Whatever is bound now is the window's framebuffer: SDL has just made
+		// the context, and on iOS that means a framebuffer object with a
+		// renderbuffer feeding the CAEAGLLayer. It is never zero, and binding
+		// zero draws nowhere -- see CFramebuffer::CreateBackbuffer, where the
+		// backbuffer is a zero framebuffer on every other platform.
+		{
+			GLint binding{0};
+			glGetIntegerv(GL_FRAMEBUFFER_BINDING, &binding);
+			device->m_DefaultFramebuffer = static_cast<GLuint>(binding);
+		}
+#endif
+
 #if OS_WIN
 		ogl_Init(SDL_GL_GetProcAddress, wutil_GetAppHDC());
 #elif (defined(SDL_VIDEO_DRIVER_X11) || defined(SDL_VIDEO_DRIVER_WAYLAND)) && !CONFIG2_GLES
